[conditions] mid-turn: proves a text sent while the agent runs a 25-second tool reaches it once, alone, character for character - queued or steered into the turn, as the agent does for a person. Cannot: when the agent records a queued prompt only at the end of the turn (Codex, Pi) it cannot show the prompt was taken at once, only that it arrived.
[conditions] owner-draft: proves a text sent while the owner's own unsent draft sits in the composer arrives in one turn after the draft, both character for character, with only spaces between - the owner's ruling of 21 October 2026, and what a person typing more gets. Cannot: how many spaces join the two is not judged: the prompt route adds one, a person types their own.
[conditions] menu-open: proves with Claude Code's question menu open, the prompt route refuses in words or delivers (never reports a lost text delivered), and on every route the next text after the menu closes arrives alone. Cannot: on the desktop routes the keys typed into the menu are the person's own - where they go is recorded, not judged. Codex and Pi have no question menu the rig can raise: Codex asks questions only in plan mode, Pi has none, and their model pickers are not used because Enter in one changes the agent's model (Codex also writes the choice to the owner's own configuration file).
[conditions] usage-limit: proves with Claude Code's usage-limit dialog on the screen, a desktop paste - a person at the terminal - reaches the agent; desktop typing is the control. Cannot: Claude Code shows the dialog only when the owner's allowance has run out, which no run can cause on demand, so the byte-echo agent draws the dialog's real words and the Director's own guard reads them. It proves what the Director lets through, not what Claude Code does with it. The prompt route is left out: whether phone and Cockpit prompts pass the dialog is the owner's open question (mission 11.6).
